      "Puerto de la Cruz was Tenerife's first tourist town, and it still feels like a real place rather than a purpose-built resort. On the lush green north coast, it is cooler, greener and more atmospheric than the south, a working town of historic plazas, a fishing harbour, grand old hotels and genuine Canarian life, with Mount Teide rising behind it.",
      "## From wine port to the island's first resort: a short history",
      "Puerto de la Cruz grew up as the harbour for the rich valley town of La Orotava, shipping the north's produce out to the world from the 16th century onwards. When a volcanic eruption destroyed the port of Garachico in 1706, Puerto de la Cruz became the most important harbour on the island. It exported first sugar and then wine, and the trade drew in Irish and British merchant families who settled around the port.",
      "Tourism came early here, too. By the late 18th century the town was one of the first places in the world to attract international visitors, drawn by its mild climate and lush setting, and the naturalist Alexander von Humboldt famously stopped here in 1799. Health and spa tourism followed through the 19th century, and by the mid-20th century Puerto de la Cruz had become the north's leading resort, long before the south was built up.",
      "## Loro Parque",
      "The town's biggest draw is Loro Parque, consistently rated one of the best animal parks in Europe, with orcas, dolphins, penguins and the world's largest indoor penguin house. It sits at the western edge of town and is an easy full day out. Book <a href=\"/en/activities/loro-parque-tickets\">Loro Parque tickets</a> in advance to skip the queue.",
      "## Beaches and Lago Martianez",
      "Because the north coast is rugged, Puerto de la Cruz built its own places to swim. The star is the Lago Martianez, a complex of seawater pools and landscaped islands designed by the Canarian artist Cesar Manrique, and a genuine landmark in its own right. For sand, Playa Jardin offers a black volcanic beach backed by gardens, with calmer water than the open coast.",
      "## Things to do and book",
      "Here is what you can book around Puerto de la Cruz, with live availability:",
      "## The old town",
      "The old town rewards slow exploration: the harbour and Plaza del Charco, the 17th-century Casa de la Aduana, hillside streets of balconied Canarian houses, and the superb Jardin Botanico with its two-century-old trees. The best way to piece it together is the self-guided <a href=\"/en/tenerife-audio-tour/puerto-de-la-cruz\">Puerto de la Cruz audio walking tour</a>, which narrates the history as you wander.",
      "## Nearby and getting there",
      "Puerto de la Cruz also makes an excellent base for the north and centre of the island. The historic town of <a href=\"/en/tenerife-audio-tour/la-orotava\">La Orotava</a> is a short hop inland, the <a href=\"/en/activities/teide-cable-car-tour\">Teide cable car</a> is reachable via the scenic north road, and the dramatic Masca valley lies to the west. It is about 40 minutes from the south resorts or the airport, and compact once you arrive. If you are touring the north, pair it with the capital <a href=\"/en/blog/santa-cruz-de-tenerife-things-to-do\">Santa Cruz</a> and UNESCO-listed <a href=\"/en/blog/la-laguna-tenerife-things-to-do\">La Laguna</a>, and find every experience on our <a href=\"/en/activities\">things to do in Tenerife</a> page.",
