    content: [
      "Los Gigantes has the most dramatic setting of any resort in Tenerife. The town sits at the foot of the Acantilados de los Gigantes, the Cliffs of the Giants, a wall of sheer volcanic rock that rises up to around 600 metres straight out of the Atlantic. It is quieter and more scenic than the big southern resorts, built around a pretty marina on the west coast.",
      "## A wall the Guanches feared: a short history",
      "Long before any resort existed, the island's original Guanche people are said to have regarded these cliffs with awe, treating the towering rock face as the edge of the world. The modern town is much newer: Los Gigantes was developed as a resort in the 1960s, helped by British investment, and expanded through the 1970s into the small marina town, in the municipality of Santiago del Teide, that you see today. The cliffs themselves, of course, have not changed in thousands of years.",
      "## Boat trips and whales",
      "The main event here is getting out on the water. Boat trips from the marina run along the base of the cliffs, an awe-inspiring perspective you simply cannot get from land, combined with whale and dolphin watching in some of the calmest, clearest water on the island and a swim stop in a hidden bay. The <a href=\"/en/activities/los-gigantes-boat-trip\">Los Gigantes cliffs boat trip</a> is the signature excursion.",
      "## Beaches",
      "The town's beach, Playa de los Guios, is a small cove of black volcanic sand directly beneath the cliffs, striking rather than sprawling. For more space, the neighbouring Playa de la Arena is a wider Blue Flag black-sand beach a few minutes away.",
      "## Things to do and book",
      "Here is what you can book from Los Gigantes, with live dates and prices:",
      "## Masca, diving and sunsets",
      "Los Gigantes is also the gateway to Masca, the remote mountain village often called Tenerife's Machu Picchu, and the spectacular <a href=\"/en/activities/masca-gorge-hike\">Masca gorge hike</a> that descends through a ravine to the sea, where a boat collects walkers and returns them to the marina. The clear, deep water makes this one of the best places on the island for <a href=\"/en/activities/scuba-diving-tenerife\">scuba diving</a>, and because the town faces west it serves up some of the finest sunsets on Tenerife.",
      "## Getting there and nearby",
      "Los Gigantes is about 40 minutes up the coast from the southern resorts, which makes it an easy day trip or a quieter base. Pair it with <a href=\"/en/blog/costa-adeje-tenerife-things-to-do\">Costa Adeje</a> for the best of the west and south, and see every bookable experience on our <a href=\"/en/activities\">things to do in Tenerife</a> page.",
    ],
